        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01WT1ZuaKEYs9YpuTB9NQZJg",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid retrieval strategies.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - pattern/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two independent sources; pattern is well-established in production RAG systems.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets\\u2014often via reciprocal rank fusion\\u2014to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity. This approach consistently outperforms either method alone on queries mixing rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs) with natural language intent. Hybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems.\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n\\n- **Complementary coverage**: Lexical search excels at exact matches and rare identifiers; dense search captures semantic relationships and paraphrases.\\n- **Robust ranking**: Reciprocal rank fusion merges results in a way that avoids over-weighting a single retrieval method\\u0027s ranking.\\n- **Production-proven**: Standard practice in modern retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems.\\n\\n## Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nReciprocal rank fusion is a technique for merging ranked result sets from multiple retrieval methods. It assigns each result a score based on the reciprocal of its rank in each ranked list, then sums these scores across all methods to produce a final ranking. This method is particularly effective in hybrid retrieval because it treats each method\\u0027s rankings as independent votes, avoiding bias toward systems with naturally higher or lower score magnitudes.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval combining sparse lexical and dense vector search strategies in production RAG systems.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
